---
# The Terraria server. Its LAN clients are the reason :7777 stays open to everyone.
#
# WHY OPEN RATHER THAN PEERED, so a later narrowing does not have to rediscover it: the Service
# is a LoadBalancer with `externalTrafficPolicy: Local`, which PRESERVES the client's real source
# address instead of SNAT-ing it to a node. That is the right setting for a game server, and it
# means the source addresses arriving here are whichever LAN or VPN devices someone plays from —
# not a set this file can enumerate. An ipBlock of the LAN prefix would be the shape to reach
# for, and it would also admit every other device on the LAN, which is most of what an attacker
# on the LAN would already have.
#
# Kuma's "k3s Terraria (game port)" monitor dials the node IP on 7777 rather than a Service name;
# the open rule covers it, so no peer is needed for the tile.
#
# 7777 is the containerPort AND the Service port — verified 2026-08-20, they match.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: terraria
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: terraria
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# Renders allow-all when the flag is false, so `netpol_baseline_enforced` is a real rollback
   lever. `| bool` for the string trap. Both explained in full in
   networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    - ports:
        - port: 7777
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
